# rule.forbid.domain-term-ambiguity

one word, one concept. a term that is overloaded — the same word used for two or more distinct senses — is ambiguous, and an ambiguous term in a contract is forbidden.

a **contract** = a dobj/dop name, an internal contract (shapes, signatures), and above all the external interfaces we publish (api, sdk, cli).

## .why

- it is the twin hazard of the synonym: a synonym is many words for one concept; an overload is one word for many. both break the vocabulary the glossary keeps
- an overloaded term forces every reader to disambiguate by context — friction on every read
- 🔴 **the overload hides an ABSENT DISTINCTION.** two concepts wear one name because the second was never granted its own word. the ambiguity is the symptom; the unnamed concept is the defect

## .the test

**"does this word name exactly ONE concept, everywhere it appears?"** one sense → adhere · two or more → the word is overloaded, and one of two repairs is owed.

| the senses differ by… | the repair |
|---|---|
| **concept** — two different things | **split.** keep the word for its primary sense; coin a term for each other sense, itemize it, record the split |
| **context** — one concept, two boundaries | **qualify** — `rule.require.boundary-qualified-terms`. a filename segment, no coinage, no dispute |

⇒ the flat namespace has one slot per word; a qualified one has as many as the domain does. so a split where a boundary was owed coins a synonym the glossary must then forbid.

🟡 the sort is the boundary rule's own question — *"$word, of WHAT?"* two answers → qualify · one answer, two referents → split.

## 🔴 .an INCONSISTENCY repair can forbid this rule's cure

`rule.forbid.domain-term-inconsistency` settles one concept named two ways onto one word. this rule grants a second word to a word that carries two concepts.

⇒ the two look adjacent, and their repairs do not compose.

measured: `lane` and `reviewer` named one concept, so `lane` became a forbidden synonym.

- but `reviewer` was *also* overloaded — the role · one run of one rubric on one guard — and `lane` was its one candidate word
- ⇒ the settlement closed the inconsistency and removed the ambiguity's only cure in the same edit

🟡 **so enumerate the senses of the word you keep BEFORE the inconsistency settles.** afterward, the word you would have reached for is forbidden.

> one word, one sense — no term does double duty 📜

blocker: a domain term overloaded across two or more distinct concepts in a contract · an inconsistency settled with no check of whether the word kept was also overloaded.
false positive: a boundary-qualified pair — `route.guard.rung` and `tool.skill.rung` are two terms, not one overloaded · generic english that composes no domain concept · a synonym, which is `rule.forbid.domain-term-synonyms`' subject.
